WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Every remote administration workflow uses an identity.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:12.520
That identity may be a personal account, service account, SSH key, deployment credential, privileged role, or managed access path.

3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:17.740
The identity determines attribution, authorization, and the impact of mistakes.

4
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.620
A shared overpowered credential is difficult to govern.

5
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:30.520
It is hard to attribute actions, hard to scope to one workflow, hard to rotate without breaking unrelated jobs, and hard to revoke during an incident.

6
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:34.940
Dedicated identities with clear purpose and ownership are easier to manage.

7
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.180
Private keys and other credentials should be protected throughout their lifecycle.

8
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:50.040
Storage location, file permissions, secret management, passphrase strategy, agent behavior, CI/CD injection, and workstation handling all shape risk.

9
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:56.300
Credentials should not drift into scripts, logs, support bundles, or shared folders.

10
00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.980
Remote permissions should match the job.

11
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.880
A script that only collects diagnostics does not need broad write privileges.

12
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:09.820
A deployment identity may need a narrow set of target actions, not general administrative power.

13
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.520
Sudo rules and command restrictions should be reviewed at a defensive level and aligned to the workflow.

14
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.320
Key inventory, rotation, and revocation are operational controls.

15
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:28.380
Teams should know who owns a key, what it is for, where it is authorized, when it was last reviewed, and how to remove it.

16
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:36.880
Stale keys and unclear authorized-key entries are signs that remote administration access is becoming unmanaged.

17
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.400
Least privilege does not make mistakes impossible, but it limits impact.

18
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:50.700
When an unexpected target, malformed input, or script bug occurs, scoped access can prevent a routine issue from becoming a wider operational event.
